WEBVTT

NOTE Dalex Software: how the studio works. Generated from the script timings by check_documentary.py.

1
00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:04.230
<v Gareth>Hiya, and welcome to Dalex Software!

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.130
<v Gareth>I'm Gareth, your guide.

3
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.790
<v Gareth>This is a software factory.

4
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:15.890
<v Gareth>Every desk is a role, every role is carried
out by AI agents, and people steer.

5
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.560
<v Gareth>Let's follow a game from the first
idea to the magazine review.

6
00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.120
<v Gareth>In we go!

7
00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.420
<v Gareth>Every game starts up here, in
commissioning.

8
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.820
<v Malcolm>Malcolm Winstanley. I
commission the games.

9
00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:33.060
<v Malcolm>An agent grills each idea,
question by question,

10
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.980
<v Malcolm>until it becomes a proper specification.

11
00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.530
<v Colin>Colin Ollerenshaw, game design.

12
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.350
<v Colin>Agents turn that spec into
requirements, a design and milestones,

13
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.270
<v Colin>and check it for gaps before
anyone builds.

14
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.940
<v Colin>The plan's on my wall.

15
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.620
<v Gareth>Down to Production, the factory floor,
where every game is built.

16
00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.430
<v Mo>I'm Mo Greenhalgh, the producer.

17
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.370
<v Mo>That board is our GitHub project,
kept live by agents.

18
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.510
<v Mo>Ask me for something, even out loud, and
my agent drafts the issue.

19
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.960
<v Mo>Nothing is sent until a
person confirms it.

20
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.710
<v Gareth>Gareth Cadwaladr, programmer.

21
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.670
<v Gareth>A coding agent writes the Z80
from the design,

22
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.840
<v Gareth>and my screen shows the code
it's working on, live.

23
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.990
<v Yvonne>I'm Yvonne Standish.

24
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.410
<v Yvonne>Agents build the studio's own tools too,

25
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.320
<v Yvonne>and my terminal shows that
code as it changes.

26
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.760
<v Vik>Vik Bassi, verification.

27
00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:37.370
<v Vik>An agent checks every build in an emulator:
frame rate, input lag, crashes.

28
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.660
<v Vik>Missing evidence is missing, not passed.

29
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.810
<v Emeka>I'm Emeka Ezeani, playtest.

30
00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.230
<v Emeka>A playtester agent plays each verified tape
and writes up what it finds.

31
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.740
<v Emeka>And you can have a go
yourself, on that telly.

32
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.130
<v Derek>Derek Tattersall, sign-off.

33
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.920
<v Derek>A separate reviewer agent checks every
milestone against its acceptance criteria

34
00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.120
<v Derek>and evidence.

35
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.690
<v Gareth>Omnibot, our courier, carries every
verified test copy over to Emeka,

36
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:06.760
<v Gareth>with a receipt.

37
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.760
[Omnibot beeps]

38
00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:11.970
<v Gareth>And that's Percy.

39
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.200
<v Gareth>Even a software factory needs a cat.

40
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.200
[Percy meows]

41
00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.520
<v Gareth>Up the stairs to Assets, where every desk
shows its owner's latest work.

42
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.170
<v Neil>Neil Whitworth, art director.

43
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.060
<v Neil>An agent sets each game's look from its
spec: a style bible, the palette,

44
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.320
<v Neil>and the master character art.

45
00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.940
<v Hyun-joo>I'm Hyun-joo Lee.

46
00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:39.470
<v Hyun-joo>Agents turn that art into Spectrum
sprites and animation.

47
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.840
<v Hyun-joo>My screen shows the newest
sprite, frame by frame.

48
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.150
<v Simon>Simon Ainscough.

49
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.430
<v Simon>Loading screens, cassette inlays and adverts,
straight from each game's media kit.

50
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.840
<v Simon>It all looks better on a telly.

51
00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.640
<v Gareth>Next door, it's audio. Listen!

52
00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.190
<v Dez>I'm Dez Mulvaney.

53
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.210
<v Dez>An agent writes the music and effects for
the Spectrum's sound chip.

54
00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:08.800
<v Dez>When the hi-fi plays one of my own songs, my
music computer follows it, bar by bar.

55
00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:12.000
<v Bev>And I'm Bev, your DJ on the hi-fi.

56
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.710
<v Bev>I introduce the records and I
give you the time.

57
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.680
<v Bev>This one's one of Dez's own!

58
00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.660
<v Gareth>Up again, to management.

59
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.210
<v Ken>Ken Ramsbottom.

60
00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.390
<v Ken>My ledger estimates what all the
AI work would cost,

61
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.190
<v Ken>and a meter downstairs shows how much of
this week's allowance is used.

62
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.220
<v Ken>Estimates, mind. Not invoices.

63
00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.640
<v Gareth>Across the yard is the
independent magazine.

64
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.670
<v Brian>Brian Openshaw, editor of Ink and Paper.

65
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.870
<v Brian>A reviewer agent plays the tape that
shipped and writes an honest review.

66
00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.370
<v Brian>My word processor types it out, and our
latest issues hang on the wall.

67
00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.320
<v Brian>We're independent of Dalex.

68
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.390
<v Gareth>So does it work?

69
00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.990
<v Gareth>Here's a worked example: Gold Master, our
own game about this very studio.

70
00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.970
<v Gareth>Specified at half past nine one evening,
then designed, built, verified,

71
00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:10.220
<v Gareth>playtested and reviewed overnight,
right through these desks.

72
00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.750
<v Emeka>That's Gold Master on the telly.

73
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.580
<v Emeka>My agent played it round after
round, all night.

74
00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:19.240
<v Mo>And there it is on the board, at
the magazine review.

75
00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.020
<v Gareth>Ink and Paper gave it seventy-two percent.

76
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.040
<v Gareth>Not bad for one night's work!

77
00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.010
<v Gareth>And you don't need to read to
use the studio.

78
00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.420
<v Gareth>There are pictures for every verb, a
microphone for questions,

79
00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:36.360
<v Gareth>and with spoken help switched on, a
narrator reads out every name,

80
00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.560
<v Gareth>message and control. Listen.

81
00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.560
<v Narrator>Mo.

82
00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.860
<v Narrator>Playtest TV.

83
00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.810
<v Gareth>So that's Dalex Software.

84
00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.540
<v Gareth>Spectrum games today, but the same roles,

85
00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.670
<v Gareth>agents and checks can build
software of any kind.

86
00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.440
<v Gareth>So, what could we build for you?
